the new occupiers of the british embassy  in kabul can't understand why the  diplomats have left and not  returned the taliban guards are happy to  be here and insist they're just  maintaining security  the taliban fighters who escorted us say  they've not touched anything  but scattered personal possessions seem  to suggest otherwise  amongst them a british regimental flag a  symbol of the thousands of soldiers who  once fought here  a flak jacket's been slung over a gate  which used to safeguard staff and a food  store room seems to have been ransacked  but the taliban still issued this  astonishing invitation to the british  whenever they want  we welcome them and we want to have  normal relations with them and our  senior officials also want this to  repair and build good relations with  them again  and for the embassies to reopen  but in the nearby canadian embassy  grounds where they found bags and bags  of empty bottles of alcohol the taliban  seemed less inviting  they must have had a lot of money to  drink this much  they used to eat good food get drunk  and then have sex with each other  a curious tortoise was still wandering  around the canadian embassy grounds and  the taliban aren't quite sure what to do  with him  here too there were signs of ransacking  or looting amidst complaints they've  been unfairly judged  where is your help and support instead  you're always shouting with terrorists  come and see the taliban government and  compare us to the previous one  analyze their security and then look at  ours  come and start your work again no one  will hurt you it's diplomatic relations  and it's the right of every country you  must do it and we must do it  the front of the american embassy is  freshly painted with taliban slogans now  and one of the first edicts issued by  the new acting government was designed  to exert more control banning protests  of descent unless permissions being  granted  two afghan journalists who filmed  demonstrations by women demanding rights  showed us their badly bruised bodies  after they were arrested and beaten for  covering what was deemed illegal  they were held separately for four hours  and say around 10 men attacked them with  cables but insist this won't stop them  reporting  has this put you off  reporting has this frightened you too  much  because it's my profession and i will  continue i won't stop i'm informing  people what's happening and no one will  stop us from doing this  it is possible i'll be killed next time  because the taliban don't have mercy on  journalists  on children women on anyone they just do  what they want they only know how to  kill and hit  but it's taliban flags on the streets  and taliban fighters enforcing the rules  and no one is stopping them  and while there's so much uncertainty  there's still a scramble to get out of  the country several hundred boarded the  first international plane to take  passengers out of afghanistan since the  american and other foreign forces left  at the end of august not everyone is  convinced by the taliban reassurances of  safety  alex crawford sky news kabul
 

